why are fast organizers called fast  in this video we'll see exactly how much  faster the so-called fast tokenizers are  compared to the slo-contour parts for  this benchmark we'll use the glue mli  data set which contains 432 000 spares  of text  we'll see how long it takes for the fast  and slow versions of a bird tokenizer to  process them all  we define our fast and slow tokenizer  using the auto tokenizer api  the first tokenizer is the default when  available so we pass along use fast  equal force to define the slow one  in a notebook we can time the execution  of the cell with some time magic command  like this  processing the wall data set is four  times faster with the fast organizer  that's weaker indeed but not very  impressive  this is because we pass along the text  to the tokenizer one at a time this is a  common mistake to do with fast  organizers which are backed by rus and  thus able to parallelize the  tokenizations of multiple texts  passing them only one text at a time is  like sending a cargo ship between two  consonants with just one container it's  very inefficient  to enrich the full speed of our fast  organizers we need to send them patches  of texts which we can do with a  particular true argument of the map  method  now those are impressive results the  fast technicians takes 12 seconds to  process the dataset that takes 4 minutes  to the slow tokenizer  summarizing the results in this table  you can see why we have called those  tokenizers fast and this is only for  tokenizing text if you ever need to  train a new tokenizer we do this very  quickly too
 

